<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaito Morcha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jaito Morcha was a protest campaign by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akali-movement">Akali movement</a> at Jaito in the princely state of Nabha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, from 1923 to 1925. It began after the British forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripudaman-singh">Ripudaman Singh</a> to abdicate, when volunteers held prayers for him at the Gangsar gurdwara. Successive bands of marchers were arrested, and in February 1924 state troops fired on a large column, killing many.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaito-morcha.jpg" alt="Jaito Morcha" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jatha_of_Akali_volunteers_marching_to_Guru-ka-Bagh_on_25_Oct_1922.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
